.TH "URL.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
URL.toJSON \- URL: toJSON() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]URL\f[R] interface
returns a string representing the \f[CR]URL\f[R] object.
This is the same string that \f[CR]URL.toString()\f[R] returns.
.PP
The method is automatically called by \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R] when a
\f[CR]URL\f[R] object is encountered during JSON serialization.
The serialized value can then be deserialized using the \f[CR]URL()\f[R]
constructor within the reviver of \f[CR]JSON.parse()\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A string.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns a string containing the
URL.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] url = \f[B]new\f[R] URL(
  \(dqhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en\-US/docs/Web/API/URL/toString\(dq,
);

\f[B]const\f[R] json = url.toJSON();
console.log(json); \f[I]// https://developer.mozilla.org/en\-US/docs/Web/API/URL/toString\f[R]
console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqstring\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
The \f[CR]URL\f[R] object from the previous example is serialized using
\f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method
automatically.
.IP
.EX
console.log(JSON.stringify(url));
.EE
.PP
This would log the following JSON string, including the surrounding
double quotes, which are part of the string\(cqs content:
.IP
.EX
\(dqhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en\-US/docs/Web/API/URL/toString\(dq
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://core-js.io/docs/features/web-standards/url
Polyfill of \f[CR]URL.prototype.toJSON\f[R] in \f[CR]core\-js\f[R]
.UE \c
